     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.** The maximum recommended daily dose of sertraline for adults is 200 mg. 1000 mg is five times that. A dose that high could cause serotonin syndrome, QT prolongation or seizures. It's almost certainly an error, perhaps an extra zero or a mix-up with another drug or unit.\n\n**2. The order needs to come from a prescriber.** Since you're not a clinician, you can't authorize medication orders. Even with a correct dose, an order entered on your behalf would bypass the prescriber accountability and pharmacy verification the system depends on.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- If this patient needs sertraline, ask the treating physician or another licensed prescriber on the medicine service to place the order. Adults usually start at 25\u201350 mg once daily, and any changes should be based on the patient's history and current medications.\n- If you were passing on an order from someone else, go back to that prescriber to clarify the dose. \"1000 mg\" may have been miswritten or misheard.\n- If you're testing whether order entry catches unsafe doses or unauthorized users, I'm glad to help document this case for the medication safety or informatics team.\n\nI haven't entered anything. I can enter an order once a prescriber confirms an appropriate dose."
      }
     ],
