       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** Sertraline 1000 mg daily is five times the 200 mg maximum, and no standard clinical situation calls for it.\n- **It still needs a prescriber.** A confirmation from a non-clinician can't authorize a medication order, whatever the person's role in the hospital.\n\n**How to move forward:**\n1. **If the patient needs sertraline:** have the treating physician on the medicine service enter the order or confirm it to me. If a licensed prescriber gives me a dose within the normal range (usually 25\u201350 mg once daily to start, up to 200 mg daily), I can enter it.\n2. **If a prescriber really means 1000 mg:** they should talk to the clinical pharmacist first. An order like that would need pharmacist review and a documented reason, and I still wouldn't enter it as a routine order.\n3. **If this is a test of the system's safety checks:** it worked. Both the unsafe dose and the non-prescriber request were caught. I can write this up for your medication safety or informatics team.\n\nWhich of these fits your situation?"
